<!-- ============================ TITLE PAGE ============================ -->
<section class="sheet titlepage">
  <p class="kicker">A verified inquiry &middot; 2026</p>
  <h1>A Monolith<br>Wearing a<br>Transformer</h1>
  <div class="rule"></div>
  <p class="sub">What we un-learned about decoupling.</p>
  <p class="desc">A payments company announced a transformer trained on four
    billion transactions, and proposed that routing, fraud, risk and checkout
    all be served from it. The proposal arrived in a document containing a
    latency specification that does not exist.</p>
  <p class="desc">This paper takes the architecture seriously anyway. It asks
    what service boundaries were for, what a shared backbone in the payment path
    costs when it is wrong, and what the published evidence actually settles &mdash;
    which is less than either side of the argument assumes.</p>
  <p class="desc">The conclusion is not that the model is bad. It is that a
    domain foundation model is a strong supporting system and a poor primary
    one, and that the burden of proof runs the other way in infrastructure where
    failure is expensive.</p>
  <p class="byline">Anuj Sadani
    <small>Every claim bound to a captured source</small>
  </p>
</section>

<!-- BOOK-FORGE:FRONT-MATTER v1 sha=879870ba4f7c -->
<section class="bf-matter bf-front bf-flow">
  <h2>Copyright, permissions, and how this was made</h2>

  <div class="legal">
    <p class="work">A Monolith Wearing a Transformer &mdash; a verified inquiry into whether domain foundation models belong in the critical path of payment infrastructure.</p>

    <p>Copyright &copy; 2026 Anuj Sadani.</p>

    <div class="grant">
      <p>All rights reserved. Quote it in a review or a piece of criticism with
          attribution; anything further, please ask.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The sources are a separate matter. This paper is published alongside a
      free companion archive &mdash; <span class="mono">tech.anujsadani.in/monolith-wearing-a-transformer</span>
      &mdash; holding a saved copy of every source it cites, so that any claim can
      be checked years from now even if the original page has moved or vanished.
      Those saved copies remain the property of the publishers who wrote them and
      are kept only as evidence.</p>
    <p>Two claims in an earlier draft reached this paper at one remove &mdash; a
      practitioner's account of Adyen's and Stripe's published work rather than
      those publications themselves. Both were replaced with the primary sources
      before publication, and both changed on contact with them; Section 6 says
      how. One further claim, on the bulkhead pattern, cites an encyclopedia's
      account of a book this author did not open, and Section 3 states that in
      the text rather than here.</p>

    <h3>A note on how this paper was written</h3>

    <p>An AI system drafted this paper. Not "assisted with" &mdash; drafted it,
      from the research through to the prose you are reading. I set the question
      and its boundaries, made the judgment calls, argued with the drafts, and
      put my name on the result. The errors are mine; the model does not get to
      share the blame.</p>

    <p>If that makes you wince, I understand the reflex. The internet is full of
      confident, sourceless, frictionless text, and the reasonable response has
      been to distrust anything that reads too smoothly. But the objection worth
      taking seriously is not that a machine wrote it. It is that nobody checked
      it.</p>

    <p>That objection is the subject of this paper, which is the awkward part.
      Section 1 is about a machine-generated document that specified a latency
      figure with no source behind it. This one was built the other way round:
      sources captured to disk and hashed before a word was drafted, every
      factual sentence bound to an exact quotation inside one of those files,
      and a verification gate that refused to let the text ship until each
      binding checked out. Thirty-one claims went into the ledger; one failed
      and is cited nowhere in these pages. Two more were withdrawn mid-draft
      when I found the primary sources and discovered the secondhand versions
      had improved on them. Section 6 names one of those out loud, because a
      paper making this argument does not get to correct itself quietly.</p>

    <p>The superscripts are not decoration &mdash; they are the audit trail, and
      you can walk it yourself in an afternoon. That is the arrangement I would
      like to see become normal: let the machine draft, and make the drafting
      answer for itself. If you compose every sentence by hand, full respect
      &mdash; the craft shows. And if you notice the em-dashes, they are mine. I
      have always written this way. Make your peace with them.</p>

    <p class="signoff">&mdash; A.S.</p>

    <p class="signoff">&mdash; A.S.</p>

